// ★ THE PROGRAM THE RELAY'S TEST FILES WERE IN AND WERE NOT.
//
// `tsconfig.json` next to this file is a BUILD program: it has `outDir`/`rootDir` and no
// `noEmit`, and `deploy/Dockerfile.relay` runs it (`RUN npx tsc`) to produce the production
// image. That is exactly why its `include` is a hand-listed set of 11 production entry
// points — adding `tests/**` there would write `dist/tests/*.js` into the shipped image.
//
// So the tests were in no program at all. `npm test` runs them under `tsx`, which strips
// types without checking them, and nothing imports a test file, so no test was reachable
// from any root either. relay-tests.yml's "Typecheck the relay" step ran that same emitting
// config and therefore compiled source only.
//
// MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. Turning this on found pre-existing errors, one of them live:
// `_amep-seed-test.ts` printed `built seed: 3 acts, currentHead=undefined` on every run,
// because `ExchangeState.currentHead` was replaced by `openHeads` (see amep.ts, "Old v0
// docs carried `currentHead`; readState migrates them") and no compiler was in the loop to
// say TS2339. Five more passed a `Part` with no `kind` to `EngagementEngine.open`; six were
// `@ts-expect-error` directives sitting over lines that do not error — a directive that
// reports nothing today, and that would MUTE a real error tomorrow if one of those private
// members were renamed.
//
// This is the same remedy `tsconfig.check.json` is at the repo root: a separate program that
// exists to be checked and never to be built. `noEmit` is what keeps the two apart.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "rootDir": "../.."
  },
  // `include` in a child config REPLACES the parent's — the 11 production entry points are
  // deliberately not repeated here; `tsconfig.json` already checks them, and every source
  // module a test touches is pulled in transitively through the test's own imports.
  //
  // Globs, not a list. The relay's `test` script is a hand-maintained `&&` chain and a file
  // has already been written, committed, and silently left out of it (see relay-tests.yml's
  // header on publish-gates.test.ts). A glob means a NEW test file is compiled the day it
  // lands rather than the day someone remembers this file.
  "include": [
    "tests/**/*.ts",
    "_*-test.ts",
    "playwright.config.ts"
  ],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist"]
}
